058db33311830694ac25fb11e11c6f7e1f208ad073cff16dca680"},"seconds":7.337566614151001,"seed":885341777140001372,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the point of intersection of the lines \\(3y = x\\) and \\(2x + 5y = 11\\), we start by expressing \\(x\\) in terms of \\(y\\) from the first equation. From \\(3y = x\\), we have \\(x = 3y\\).\n\nNext, we substitute \\(x = 3y\\) into the second equation \\(2x + 5y = 11\\):\n\n\\[\n2(3y) + 5y = 11\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n6y + 5y = 11\n\\]\n\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n11y = 11\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(y\\), we divide both sides by 11:\n\n\\[\ny = 1\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(y = 1\\), we substitute this value back into the equation \\(x = 3y\\) to find \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx = 3(1) = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the coordinates of point \\(A\\) are \\((3, 1)\\). To find the sum of the coordinates of point \\(A\\), we add \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n3 + 1 = 4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the coordinates of point \\(A\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:900"}
